WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.718
Validation of the initial target does not
authorize the rest of an HTTP exchange.

2
00:00:04.798 --> 00:00:07.589
A redirect can replace the protocol,
hostname, port,

3
00:00:07.669 --> 00:00:09.778
and therefore the network destination.

4
00:00:09.858 --> 00:00:14.137
Relative locations still produce a new
absolute target when resolved against the

5
00:00:14.217 --> 00:00:14.900
current URL.

6
00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:18.205
If redirects are unnecessary, disable
automatic following.

7
00:00:18.285 --> 00:00:19.401
If they are required,

8
00:00:19.481 --> 00:00:23.761
bound the chain and treat every hop as a
fresh destination decision before the next

9
00:00:23.841 --> 00:00:24.523
connection.

10
00:00:24.603 --> 00:00:28.573
For each redirect, resolve the Location
value against the current URL with a

11
00:00:28.653 --> 00:00:32.995
standards-aware parser, then repeat
protocol, credential, hostname, port, DNS,

12
00:00:33.075 --> 00:00:33.819
and IP policy.

13
00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:36.690
Reject malformed locations, loops,
excessive hops,

14
00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.313
and any transition outside the feature's
rules.

15
00:00:39.393 --> 00:00:43.487
Do not assume that the first approved
hostname confers trust on a later target.

16
00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:47.784
Method changes and sensitive header
forwarding also require deliberate client

17
00:00:47.864 --> 00:00:49.353
behavior across redirects.

18
00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:52.348
DNS creates another boundary between
checking and use.

19
00:00:52.428 --> 00:00:56.708
A design may resolve a hostname for
validation and then pass the hostname to an HTTP

20
00:00:56.788 --> 00:00:59.145
client that performs an independent
lookup.

21
00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:02.636
Those resolutions are not guaranteed to
return the same address.

22
00:01:02.716 --> 00:01:04.639
The defensive property is stronger:

23
00:01:04.719 --> 00:01:08.378
the connection must use an address that
passed the policy for that hop.

24
00:01:08.458 --> 00:01:12.738
Achieving it may require a controlled
lookup callback, a vetted HTTP dispatcher,

25
00:01:12.818 --> 00:01:14.927
a proxy that enforces the same decision,

26
00:01:15.007 --> 00:01:19.100
or another transport mechanism that binds
approved resolution to connection

27
00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:20.049
establishment.

28
00:01:20.129 --> 00:01:24.346
Do not implement that binding by casually
replacing every URL hostname with an IP

29
00:01:24.426 --> 00:01:24.922
address.

30
00:01:25.002 --> 00:01:28.662
HTTPS still needs the original hostname
for server-name indication,

31
00:01:28.742 --> 00:01:31.347
certificate verification, and virtual
hosting,

32
00:01:31.427 --> 00:01:33.784
and HTTP Host semantics must remain
correct.

33
00:01:33.864 --> 00:01:38.268
The transport should preserve those
identity checks while constraining the socket

34
00:01:38.348 --> 00:01:39.092
destination.

35
00:01:39.172 --> 00:01:43.265
Network egress rules provide an
independent backstop when library behavior,

36
00:01:43.345 --> 00:01:46.509
races, or configuration drift defeat an
application check.

37
00:01:46.589 --> 00:01:50.124
Redirect validation and DNS-to-connection
binding are distinct,

38
00:01:50.204 --> 00:01:52.127
but both enforce the same principle:

39
00:01:52.207 --> 00:01:55.060
authorize the destination actually used at
every hop.
